WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.660
Detection engineering is the discipline of creating and maintaining visibility into suspicious,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.180
risky, or policy-relevant activity.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.600
Detection engineers design capabilities that help organizations identify threats,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.360
investigate alerts,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.160
and improve defensive awareness before small signals become larger problems.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.960
Although people often associate detection engineering with writing SIEM queries,

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.860
the discipline is much broader.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.280
It includes telemetry planning, threat-informed design, testing, documentation, tuning,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.560
lifecycle management, measurement, and continuous improvement.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.980
A detection is not just a line of logic.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.300
It is a maintained defensive asset that depends on data quality, context, alert presentation,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.340
investigation guidance, ownership, and a plan for review.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.000
If any of those pieces are missing,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.040
analysts may receive noise instead of useful signal.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.980
Detection engineering gives security teams a repeatable way to ask important defensive questions.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.120
What activity matters?

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.020
What data can show it?

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.300
How will the alert be tested?

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.960
What should analysts do when it fires?

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.140
How will the team know whether the detection is still useful?

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.660
The purpose is practical.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.840
Detection engineering helps defenders turn messy operational data into meaningful visibility,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.500
so teams can investigate faster, prioritize better,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.920
and learn from what they observe in their environment.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.340
A mature detection engineering program also creates shared language.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.660
Instead of debating alerts one at a time, teams can discuss coverage, fidelity, ownership,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.700
dependencies, review dates, response guidance, and measurable outcomes.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.500
That shift matters because security operations is under constant pressure.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.060
Detection engineering gives teams a structured way to improve visibility while respecting

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.960
the limits of analyst time,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.860
platform performance, and business context.
